    { label: "Passage 1", title: "PASSAGE 1 – The Changing World of Work", html: `
      <p>Over the past two decades, the world of work has changed considerably. Technological developments, particularly the growth of the internet and digital communication tools, have transformed how people perform their jobs. In the past, most employees were expected to work in traditional offices, following fixed schedules and meeting their colleagues face to face. Today, however, many companies allow their employees to work remotely, giving them greater flexibility over where and when they complete their tasks.</p>
      <p>One major advantage of remote working is that employees can save time and money by avoiding daily journeys to the workplace. This can be especially beneficial for people who live far from their offices or have family responsibilities. Furthermore, working from home may create a more comfortable environment, allowing individuals to organise their working hours around personal commitments. Some studies have also suggested that employees who have greater control over their schedules may experience higher levels of job satisfaction.</p>
      <p>Nevertheless, remote working is not without its challenges. Some employees find it difficult to separate their professional and personal lives when their home becomes their workplace. Without clear boundaries, they may continue answering emails or completing tasks long after their official working hours have ended. In addition, working remotely can reduce opportunities for informal communication. In traditional offices, employees often exchange ideas during lunch breaks or casual conversations, which can contribute to stronger professional relationships.</p>
      <p>Another concern is the potential effect of remote working on teamwork. Although digital platforms make it possible to communicate across different locations, online meetings cannot always replace face-to-face interaction. Misunderstandings may occur more frequently when people cannot observe body language or immediately clarify what others mean. As a result, managers need to develop effective communication strategies to ensure that all team members remain informed and involved.</p>
      <p>Rather than completely replacing traditional offices, many experts believe that a combination of remote and office-based work may offer the most practical solution. This approach, commonly known as hybrid working, allows employees to work from home on certain days and attend the office when necessary. It provides flexibility while maintaining opportunities for collaboration. Ultimately, the success of any working arrangement depends on the nature of the job, the needs of employees and the ability of organisations to manage their workforce effectively.</p>
    `},
