      <p>The rapid development of technology has created a growing demand for digital skills in the modern workplace. Employees are increasingly expected to use computer software, communicate through online platforms and analyse information effectively. Consequently, learning new skills has become an essential part of professional development.</p>
      <p>Many organisations now provide online training programmes to help their employees improve their abilities. These programmes often include video lessons, interactive activities and practical assignments. Unlike traditional classroom courses, online training allows participants to study at their own pace and access learning materials from different locations.</p>
      <p>However, online learning requires a considerable degree of self-discipline. Without a teacher physically present, some learners may struggle to remain motivated or complete their assignments on time. To address this issue, certain organisations introduce weekly deadlines and progress reports. These measures encourage employees to maintain consistent study habits.</p>
      <p>Another important factor is the practical application of knowledge. Simply watching instructional videos may not be enough to develop professional competence. Employees need opportunities to apply what they have learned to real workplace situations. For instance, someone studying data analysis should practise working with actual datasets rather than only memorising theoretical definitions.</p>
      <p>Ultimately, continuous learning benefits both individuals and organisations. Employees who regularly update their skills may become more confident and adaptable, while companies can build a workforce that is better prepared for technological changes.</p>
    `},
    { label: "Passage 4", title: "PASSAGE 4 – The Importance of Public Libraries", html: `
      <p>Public libraries have played an important role in communities for centuries. Traditionally, they were mainly places where people could borrow books, read newspapers and access educational materials. Today, however, their responsibilities have expanded considerably.</p>
      <p>Modern libraries provide a wide range of services beyond lending printed books. Many offer free internet access, computer facilities and digital resources. These services are particularly valuable for people who cannot afford personal computers or reliable internet connections at home. As a result, libraries help reduce the digital divide between different groups in society.</p>
      <p>Libraries also serve as community centres where people can participate in educational and cultural activities. For example, some organise language classes, reading clubs and workshops for children and adults. Such activities encourage lifelong learning and create opportunities for people to interact with others who share similar interests.</p>
      <p>Despite these benefits, public libraries face several challenges. One major issue is limited funding, which can make it difficult to purchase new materials, maintain facilities and introduce modern technology. Furthermore, the increasing popularity of online information sources has led some people to question whether traditional libraries are still necessary.</p>
      <p>Nevertheless, libraries continue to offer something that digital platforms cannot fully replace: a shared physical space for learning, communication and community participation. Their future success will depend on their ability to adapt to changing needs while maintaining their fundamental educational mission.</p>
    `}
