    { label: "Passage 1", title: "PASSAGE 1 – Understanding the Problem of Food Waste", html: `
      <p>Food waste has become a significant environmental and social issue around the world. It occurs at different stages of the food supply chain, including production, transportation, retail and consumption. According to the United Nations Environment Programme (UNEP), approximately 1.05 billion tonnes of food waste were generated worldwide in 2022. This figure includes parts of food that are not normally eaten. The amount represents almost one-fifth of the food available to consumers.</p>
      <p>Households are responsible for a particularly large proportion of this problem, accounting for approximately 60% of total food waste. One common reason is that people purchase more food than they actually need. Special offers and attractive packaging may encourage customers to buy unnecessary products. In addition, some families prepare excessively large portions or forget about ingredients stored in their refrigerators. Consequently, food may spoil before anyone has the opportunity to consume it.</p>
      <p>Another source of confusion involves food date labels. Many consumers misunderstand the difference between a “use-by” date and a “best-before” date. A use-by date is generally related to food safety, whereas a best-before date concerns the expected quality of a product. Therefore, food does not automatically become unsafe immediately after its best-before date, although consumers should always consider storage conditions and signs of spoilage before deciding whether to eat it.</p>
      <p>Fortunately, there are several practical ways to reduce avoidable food waste. Families can check their refrigerators before shopping, prepare weekly meal plans and create shopping lists. Buying only what is necessary and storing food correctly can also make a considerable difference. Furthermore, suitable leftovers can be frozen or used in other meals rather than immediately thrown away. These household habits may seem simple, but they can help prevent large quantities of food from being wasted.</p>
      <p>Businesses and governments also have important responsibilities. Supermarkets can offer discounts on products approaching their use-by dates, while restaurants can adjust portion sizes to reduce uneaten food. In some situations, safe surplus food can be donated to organisations that support people in need. Governments can collect information about food waste to identify major problem areas and measure progress over time. Ultimately, reducing food waste requires cooperation between consumers, businesses and public authorities rather than relying on individuals alone.</p>
      <p class="source">Factual background adapted and paraphrased from UNEP’s Food Waste Index Report 2024.</p>
    `},
    { label: "Passage 2", title: "PASSAGE 2 – Four Volunteers Making Their Cities Greener", html: `
      <p>Four young people describe their experiences of participating in environmental projects in their communities.</p>
      <div class="person"><h4>A. Anna – Community Garden Volunteer</h4><p>I joined a community garden project because I have always been interested in growing vegetables. Every Sunday, I work with other residents to plant, water and harvest different kinds of food. What I enjoy most is the opportunity to teach children how vegetables grow and why locally produced food is important. I believe these activities can encourage younger generations to develop healthier habits.</p></div>
      <div class="person"><h4>B. Ben – Urban Tree Monitoring Volunteer</h4><p>My project focuses on monitoring trees along city streets. Instead of mainly planting trees, our team records their locations, identifies different species and checks their general condition. We use a smartphone application to collect information and create digital maps. I particularly enjoy working with technology and discovering how environmental data can help local authorities make better decisions.</p></div>
      <div class="person"><h4>C. Chloe – River Clean-up Volunteer</h4><p>I spend most Saturday mornings participating in river clean-up activities. Our group collects plastic bottles, food packaging and other rubbish along the riverbanks. We also separate recyclable materials from general waste. One of my main concerns is that rubbish may block drainage systems, particularly during periods of heavy rainfall. I hope our work can help make the surrounding environment cleaner and safer.</p></div>
      <div class="person"><h4>D. Daniel – Rooftop Farming Volunteer</h4><p>I recently joined a rooftop farming programme organised by a local environmental organisation. We maintain vegetable beds on top of a community building and learn how to manage irrigation systems. I am interested in developing a career in landscape management, so this project offers valuable practical experience. It also allows me to understand how unused urban spaces can become productive areas.</p></div>
    `},
    { label: "Passage 3", title: "PASSAGE 3 – Why Urban Trees Matter", html: `
      <p>Urban trees are much more than decorative features in a city. They provide environmental, economic and social benefits that contribute to the quality of life of residents. As cities become more crowded, green spaces are increasingly important for creating healthier and more sustainable living environments.</p>
      <p>One major benefit of urban trees is their ability to reduce high temperatures. By providing shade, trees cool nearby buildings, roads and other surfaces that would otherwise absorb large amounts of sunlight. This can help reduce the urban heat island effect, a situation in which built-up areas experience higher temperatures than surrounding rural locations. As a result, well-planned tree cover can make certain neighbourhoods more comfortable during hot weather.</p>
      <p>Trees also help manage water in urban environments. Their leaves and branches intercept and temporarily store rainfall, reducing the amount of water that reaches the ground at one time. Their roots can strengthen soil and help reduce erosion. These functions are particularly useful in areas that experience heavy rainfall and may be vulnerable to flooding or landslides.</p>
      <p>Another important contribution is the protection of urban biodiversity. Green corridors and urban parks provide habitats and movement routes for different species of plants and animals. Trees can also help absorb certain air pollutants and reduce environmental noise. In addition, parks and green areas offer opportunities for walking, sports, recreation and environmental education. These activities can encourage physical activity and support residents’ physical and psychological well-being.</p>
      <p>However, simply planting a large number of trees does not guarantee that a city will become environmentally sustainable. Trees must be selected carefully according to local conditions, available space and water resources. Furthermore, they require proper maintenance throughout their lives. Urban planners should work with environmental experts and local residents to identify priorities and ensure that green infrastructure responds to local needs. Effective planning can help cities enjoy the environmental and social benefits of urban trees for many years.</p>
      <p class="source">Factual background adapted and paraphrased from FAO materials on urban and peri-urban forestry.</p>
    `},
    { label: "Passage 4", title: "PASSAGE 4 – Repair, Reuse and the Circular Economy", html: `
      <p>For many years, people have followed a simple pattern of consumption: buy a product, use it and throw it away when it becomes damaged or outdated. However, this approach creates large amounts of waste and increases the demand for new materials. An alternative is the circular economy, which aims to keep products and resources in use for as long as possible. Repairing, maintaining and reusing existing products are important ways to achieve this goal.</p>
      <p>Repairing a damaged product can extend its useful lifespan and reduce the consumption of natural resources. For example, repairing a broken chair may be more sustainable than replacing it with a completely new one. Similarly, replacing a small part of an electronic device may allow the entire product to continue functioning. These actions can reduce unnecessary waste and decrease the environmental impact associated with manufacturing new goods.</p>
      <p>In recent years, community repair events have become a practical way to encourage this behaviour. Sometimes called repair cafés, these events bring together volunteers with different technical abilities and residents who have damaged household items. Participants may bring clothes, bicycles, small electrical appliances or furniture. Volunteers help identify problems and demonstrate simple repair techniques. In the process, residents can develop practical repair skills and share knowledge with other members of the community.</p>
      <p>Nevertheless, repairing products is not always straightforward. Some items require specialised technical knowledge, while others may be difficult to fix because suitable spare parts are unavailable. In certain cases, the cost of repair may also be higher than the price of purchasing a replacement. For this reason, governments and manufacturers can support the circular economy by improving access to repair information, making spare parts easier to obtain and encouraging the production of durable goods.</p>
      <p>Ultimately, a successful circular economy requires changes in both consumer behaviour and business practices. Rather than immediately replacing damaged products, people should consider whether repair or reuse is a suitable option. Although these methods cannot solve every environmental problem, they can help reduce waste and encourage more responsible consumption.</p>
      <p class="source">Factual background adapted and paraphrased from the European Environment Agency’s overview of the repair sector.</p>
    `}
  ],
