    { label: "Passage 1", title: "PASSAGE 1 – Rethinking the Way We Travel", html: `
      <p>Tourism is an important industry in many countries. It creates employment, supports local businesses and gives visitors opportunities to experience different cultures. However, the rapid growth of tourism can also create difficulties, particularly in destinations that receive large numbers of visitors throughout the year.</p>
      <p>One common problem is overcrowding. When too many tourists visit a popular destination at the same time, public spaces may become crowded and local transport systems may struggle to meet demand. In some places, the cost of accommodation and everyday goods may also increase, making life more expensive for local residents.</p>
      <p>The natural environment can be affected as well. Beaches may become polluted, wildlife habitats may be disturbed and historic sites may suffer physical damage. These problems can reduce the quality of the visitor experience and make destinations less attractive in the long term.</p>
      <p>To address these challenges, many organisations promote sustainable tourism. This approach aims to balance three important areas: environmental protection, economic development and respect for local cultures. It does not mean that people should stop travelling. Instead, it encourages tourists, businesses and governments to make more responsible decisions.</p>
      <p>Individual travellers can contribute in several ways. They can choose locally owned accommodation, purchase products made by local craftspeople and use public transport when possible. They should also respect local customs, avoid leaving rubbish behind and follow instructions when visiting protected natural areas.</p>
      <p>Tourism companies have responsibilities too. They can employ local workers, reduce unnecessary waste and provide information about responsible behaviour. Governments, meanwhile, need to protect important natural and cultural sites and ensure that local communities benefit from tourism development.</p>
      <p>Some people believe that sustainable tourism is mainly about protecting the environment. However, this view is incomplete. A destination cannot be considered truly sustainable if its natural attractions are protected but local residents receive few economic benefits or have no influence over important decisions.</p>
      <p>Ultimately, successful tourism requires cooperation. Visitors should be aware of their impact, businesses should operate responsibly and governments should create effective policies. When these groups work together, tourism can continue to provide benefits without destroying the qualities that make a destination special.</p>
    `},
