    { label: "Passage 3", title: "PASSAGE 3 – Understanding Coral Reefs", html: `
      <p>Coral reefs are among the most diverse ecosystems in the ocean. They provide habitats for thousands of marine species, including fish, sea turtles and other organisms. They are also important to people because they support fishing, tourism and other economic activities. In addition, coral reefs help protect coastlines by reducing the force of waves during storms.</p>
      <p>Despite their importance, coral reefs face several threats. One of the most serious is rising ocean temperature. When water becomes too warm, corals may experience stress and expel the tiny algae living inside their tissues. This process is known as coral bleaching. Without these algae, corals lose much of their colour and an important source of food.</p>
      <p>Bleaching does not always mean that corals are dead. If the stressful conditions do not last too long, some corals can recover. However, when high temperatures continue for an extended period, corals may become weak and eventually die.</p>
      <p>Human activities also contribute to reef damage. Pollution from land can enter the ocean through rivers and rainwater. Unsustainable fishing practices may remove important marine species, while careless visitors can physically damage corals by touching them or stepping on them.</p>
      <p>Scientists and conservation organisations are working to protect coral reefs. They monitor reef conditions, study coral health and develop restoration projects. Local communities can also help by reducing pollution, following responsible diving practices and supporting marine protection programmes.</p>
      <p>Although global climate change remains a major threat, reducing local sources of damage can improve the ability of coral reefs to survive stressful conditions. Protecting these ecosystems requires long-term cooperation between governments, scientists, businesses and local residents.</p>
    `},
    { label: "Passage 4", title: "PASSAGE 4 – Sleep and the Learning Process", html: `
      <p>Many students believe that studying for longer hours is the best way to prepare for examinations. Some even stay awake late at night to review lessons, hoping to remember more information. However, sleep plays an important role in learning and should not be treated as wasted time.</p>
      <p>During the day, the brain receives and processes a large amount of information. While people sleep, the brain continues working. Certain stages of sleep help organise newly learned information and form memories. This process allows some information to become more stable and easier to recall later.</p>
      <p>Sleep is also closely connected to concentration. Students who do not get enough sleep may find it difficult to pay attention in class, understand complicated ideas or make decisions. Even if they spend many hours studying, their performance may be affected by tiredness.</p>
      <p>Developing healthy sleeping habits can improve the quality of sleep. For example, going to bed and waking up at regular times helps establish a consistent routine. Students should also reduce their caffeine consumption in the evening and avoid using electronic devices immediately before bedtime.</p>
      <p>The amount of sleep required can vary depending on a person’s age and individual needs. Therefore, there is no single sleeping schedule that works perfectly for everyone. Nevertheless, getting sufficient sleep, studying regularly and taking suitable breaks can help students learn more effectively.</p>
    `}
